  "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\u2032} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{\u2032}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x}+(\\beta +6\\beta ){e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x}+7\\beta {e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-6x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,7\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}.\n$$",
